{"result":"*******************************************************************************\n Revised: Sep 30, 2026              BepiColombo / (Sun)                    -121\n                            http://sci.esa.int/bepicolombo/ \n \n LAUNCH DATE:                                            MERCURY ARRIVAL DATE:\n  2018-Oct-20 01:45 UTC                                      2026-Nov         \n\n  Earth flyby: 2020-Apr-10, 04:24:58 UTC @ 19066 km, 352.85 deg E,-19.70 deg S\n\n LAUNCH VEHICLE:                                         LAUNCH SITE:\n  Ariane 5                                                Kourou, French Guiana\n\n Note (2024-Sep-02): Due to unexpected electric currents between MTM's solar \n  arrays and the power extraction and distribution unit, electric propulsion \n  is reduced, requiring a one-year delay in Mercury arrival, to November 2026. \n\n BACKGROUND:\n  ESA's BepiColombo mission to orbit Mercury for one Earth year (four Mercury\n  years) is composed of two spacecraft: the ESA Mercury Planetary Orbiter (MPO)\n  and the ISAS/JAXA Mercury Magnetospheric Orbiter (MMO).\n\n  MMO will be released once the MMO orbit is reached. MPO will be inserted \n  using chemical propulsion.\n\n   1) MPO will study the surface and internal composition of Mercury \n   2) MMO will study the region around the planet influenced by its \n      magnetic field\n\n  For the 8.2 year launch & cruise phases, MPO and MMO will be carried as part \n  of the Mercury Composite Spacecraft and released on arrival at Mercury. \n\n  There will be nine gravity assists during the cruise phase: \n\n   2020-Apr-10: Earth flyby\n   2020-Oct-15: Venus flyby\n   2021-Aug-11: Venus Flyby\n   2021-Oct-01: First Mercury flyby\n   2022-Jun-23: 2nd Mercury flyby\n   2023-Jun-20: 3rd Mercury flyby\n   2024-Sep-04: 4th Mercury flyby\n   2024-Dec-01: 5th Mercury flyby\n   2025-Jan-08: 6th Mercury flyby\n   ....\n   2026-Nov   : Mercury orbit insertion\n   2028-Apr   : End of nominal mission\n   2029-Apr   : End of extended mission\n\n OBJECTIVES:\n  Study and understand the composition, geophysics, atmosphere, magnetosphere \n  and history of Mercury with following science objectives:\n\n   * Investigate origin and evolution of a planet close to the parent star\n   * Study Mercury form, interior structure, geology, composition and craters\n   * Examine composition and dynamics of Mercury's vestigial atmosphere\n   * Probe Mercury's magnetized envelope; structure and dynamics\n   * Determine the origin of Mercury's magnetic field\n   * Investigate polar deposits: their composition and origin\n   * Perform a test of Einstein's theory of general relativity\n \n SPACECRAFT PHYSICAL PROPERTIES:\n  Total launch mass  = 4100 kg\n\n                         MPO                 MMO\n  In Mercury orbit     1150 kg             275 kg  \n  Payload mass           80 kg              45 kg\n  Payload power        100-150 W            90 W\n  Data volume          1550 Gbits/year     160 Gbits/year\n  Average data rate    50 kbits/s            5 kbits/s\n  Antenna              1-m X/Ka-band       0.8 m X-band\n  Orientation          Nadir pointing      Spin axis at 90 deg. to Sun\n  Stabilisation        3-axis stabilized   15-rpm spin-stabilised \n\n BASIC ORBIT PARAMETERS AT MERCURY:\n                       MPO (polar)        MMO (polar)\n  peri/apo apses      480 x 1500 km      590 x 11640 km\n  period                 2.3 hr            9.3 hr\n\n SPACECRAFT TRAJECTORY: \n  Trajectory from ESA. Data fit through 2026-Sep-28, prediction thereafter.\n\n   Name                                    Start (TDB)          Stop (TDB)\n   --------------------------------------  -----------------  -----------------\n   bc_mpo_fcp_00236_20181020_20270411_v01  2018-Oct-20 02:14  2027-Apr-10 23:59\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Tue